"OpenAI opens Singapore AI lab as IMDA updates AI framework OpenAI establishes its first overseas applied AI research center in Singapore, and the Singapore government updates its agent AI governance framework. OpenAI establishes its first applied AI research center outside the US in Singapore, investing more than S$300 million. In collaboration with the Singapore government, we operate AI-related technology job creation, talent training, and startup accelerator programs. The Government of Singapore (IMDA) has updated its governance framework to enhance risk management and accountability for agent AI systems. Norway’s $2.3 trillion wealth fund objects to John Elkann’s reappointment to Meta’s board Norway's sovereign wealth fund supported the shareholder proposal, citing concerns about Meta's board composition and management of AI data privacy. The Norwegian Government Pension Fund, the world's largest sovereign wealth fund, withheld a vote on John Elkann's re-appointment as Meta director, citing non-participation. The fund voted in favor of a total of five proposals, including a shareholder proposal calling for a data privacy impact assessment of Meta's AI chatbot. Norway's sovereign wealth fund largely follows management's recommendations, but this time it took a rare stand against Meta, revealing its misgivings about Meta's governance. London’s Fresha hits unicorn status with KKR-led $80M raise Fresha, a London-based beauty and wellness booking platform, has become a unicorn with a corporate value of over $1 billion, attracting $80 million in investment led by KKR. Fresha received an investment of $80 million from KKR's Next Generation Technology Growth Fund, valuing the company at over $1 billion. More than 130,000 beauty and wellness businesses currently use the platform, processing more than 35 million bookings each month and generating $15 billion in annual transaction volume. This investment is planned to be used for future global business expansion and investment in AI technology. Kawasaki Heavy ties up with Nvidia on physical AI, and the rideable robot horse gets a foundation model Kawasaki Heavy Industries has decided to cooperate with NVIDIA and major technology companies to establish a physical AI development base for robots and apply the foundation model to the quadrupedal robot 'CORLEO'. Kawasaki Heavy Industries establishes a joint development base for physical AI robot development with NVIDIA, Analog Devices, Microsoft, and Fujitsu in San Jose, USA. As the first collaborative project, we plan to apply NVIDIA's simulation tools and foundation model to the off-road quadruped robot 'CORLEO'. This collaboration accelerates the development of robots in the medical, nursing, and mobility fields, and is part of efforts to introduce physical AI to address Japan's aging population. Austrian startup REPS raises $23.6m to turn road traffic into electricity Austrian startup REPS has attracted $23.6 million in investment for its technology that converts braking energy from vehicles on the road into electricity. REPS has developed energy harvesting technology that converts the energy and heat generated when a vehicle brakes into electricity through devices installed on the road. Through this investment, we plan to expand the application of technology to ports, logistics hubs, and cities. It is currently undergoing pilot testing in the German port of Hamburg and is claimed to be 254 times more efficient than existing market products. Sora shutdown leaves Critterz at the Cannes market without its model Due to the suspension of OpenAI's Sora model service, the AI-generated animation film 'Critterz' failed to achieve its official debut goal at the Cannes Film Festival, causing a disruption in the production pipeline. As OpenAI discontinued the Sora model service last March, the movie 'Critterz', which was being produced based on the model, is experiencing great disruption in production. As a result, 'Critterz' failed to be screened officially at the Cannes Film Festival, which was its original goal, and alternative production tools are also uncertain. OpenAI is terminating its Sora service due to high operating costs and user decline, and is shifting its future video research toward global simulation work for robotics. The literary world isn’t prepared for AI It addresses the verification difficulties and reliability issues facing the publishing industry as works suspected of being written by AI are nominated for literary awards. Allegations have been raised that Commonwealth Short Story Prize shortlisted Jamir Nazir's 'The Serpent in the Grove' was written by AI. Experts point out that because AI learns human writing, it is becoming increasingly difficult to completely distinguish between human and AI writing. The publishing industry lacks clear standards and preparations to verify the originality of works in line with the pace of development of AI writing technology. Why would you disrespect your favorite artist with an AI remix? This article addresses concerns that the agreement between Spotify and Universal Music Group (UMG) to introduce AI remix and cover creation tools could undermine artists' creativity and copyright. Spotify is collaborating with Universal Music Group to launch an AI-based remix and cover song creation tool using the company's catalog. Although the tool is positioned as a premium subscription service for 'superfans', the article sees it as disrespectful to artists and creativity. They criticized that the results generated by users inputting prompts to AI are not true artistic communication or creative acts. Samsung&#8217;s memory chip employees negotiated $340,000 bonuses this year Samsung Electronics has reached a provisional agreement that includes large-scale bonus payments to prevent a strike by employees in the semiconductor division. Samsung Electronics reached a provisional agreement to prevent a strike that had been predicted by employees in the semiconductor division. The agreement includes a cash incentive payment equivalent to 50% of the basic annual salary and a stock incentive payment using 10.5% of annual operating profit. This performance bonus is conditional on whether the company achieves profits, and is distributed to all employees in the semiconductor division as well as the memory division. Microsoft Releases Fara1.5: A Family of Browser Computer-Use Agents (4B/9B/27B) That Outperform OpenAI Operator and Gemini 2.5 Computer Use on Online-Mind2Web Microsoft has released 'Fara1.5', a computer-based agent model family that runs in web browsers. Fara1.5 was released in three sizes: 4B, 9B, and 27B, and showed higher performance than OpenAI's Operator and Gemini 2.5 Computer Use in the Online-Mind2Web benchmark. The model operates in an 'observe-think-act' loop, analyzing browser screenshots and performing mouse and keyboard actions. For training, we built a synthetic data pipeline called FaraGen1.5 to generate data in a synthetic environment (FaraEnvs) for open Internet tasks and secure gated-domain tasks. Build Recurrent-Depth Transformers with OpenMythos for MLA, GQA, Sparse MoE, and Loop-Scaled Reasoning This is a technical tutorial that builds a cyclic depth transformer model using the OpenMythos library and tests the performance of various attention mechanisms such as MLA and GQA and the inference ability through cyclic loops. We cover how to use OpenMythos to implement MLA and GQA style transformer models and compare parameter counts. The stability of the model is evaluated by calculating the spectral radius of the circular injection matrix. We demonstrate through synthetic inference tasks how a single model can reuse parameters through a circular loop to perform deeper computations. Easy Agentic Tool Calling with Gemma 4 We'll show you how to use Gemma 4 to implement true agent functionality, including browsing the local file system and running the Python interpreter. We present a method of building a true 'agent' model that moves away from the use of existing web API-based tools and interacts with the local environment. We provide Gemma 4 models with a sandboxed file system exploration tool and a limited Python interpreter tool, allowing the model to determine its own environment and call tools. Covers how to run agent loops reliably in the local environment using the gemma4:e2b edge transformation model. SOLAR: A Self-Optimizing Open-Ended Autonomous Agent for Lifelong Learning and Continual Adaptation Research proposing SOLAR, a new autonomous agent capable of continuous learning and adaptation in a dynamic environment We developed the SOLAR agent to solve the concept drift and adaptation cost problems experienced by large-scale language models (LLMs). Self-optimizes and discovers adaptive strategies at test time using parameter-level meta-learning and multi-level reinforcement learning Maintaining a balance between adaptability to new tasks and maintaining existing meta-knowledge through an evolving knowledge base Tool-Augmented Agent for Closed-loop Optimization,Simulation,and Modeling Orchestration To bridge the gap between industrial design and simulation, we propose COSMO-Agent, a tool-based reinforcement learning framework that automates the CAD-CAE process. The CAD creation, CAE solving, result parsing, and geometric modification processes are organized into an interactive reinforcement learning environment, and LLM learns to orchestrate them. We design a multi-constraint compensation scheme that comprehensively promotes feasibility, toolchain robustness, and output validity. We built industry-standard datasets for 25 parts categories to support model training and evaluation. OSCToM: RL-Guided Adversarial Generation for High-Order Theory of Mind This study proposed the OSCToM framework, which models the conflict between observers' and one's own beliefs, to improve LLM's ability to reason in complex social situations. We developed the OSCToM model to improve theory of mind (ToM) reasoning ability in complex and recursive social situations, which is a limitation of existing LLM. By combining reinforcement learning (RL), domain-specific language, and constructive agency models, we create and learn complex belief conflict situations with information asymmetry. The OSCToM-8B model achieves 76% accuracy on the FANToM benchmark and also increases data generation efficiency by 6x. AgentCo-op: Retrieval-Based Synthesis of Interoperable Multi-Agent Workflows We introduce ‘AgentCo-op’, a discovery-based workflow synthesis framework that combines different AI agents and tools to automate complex open-world tasks. AgentCo-op synthesizes reusable technologies, tools, and external agents into executable workflows through type-specific artifact delivery. In case of execution failure, it has the ability to partially fix the problematic component according to its own guide. It performed well in six benchmark tests and improved cost-effectiveness compared to existing multi-agent baselines. High Quality Embeddings for Horn Logic Reasoning A study that proposed a new embedding learning technique that quantifies logical sentences to increase the efficiency of Horn Logic inference. We explore how to accelerate the reasoning process by using neural networks to determine the ranking of options for a logical reasoner. An efficient method for learning embeddings based on triplet loss is introduced. We propose a strategy to optimize the balance by level of difficulty (easy, medium, hard) when configuring learning data and to focus more on difficult examples during the learning process. Teaching Language Models to Forecast Research Success Through Comparative Idea Evaluation This is research on a new methodology in which a language model compares and evaluates and predicts the likelihood of success of research ideas before conducting actual experiments. We developed a model to predict, without experimentation, which of the numerous research ideas generated by AI will yield better results. Using objective data from PapersWithCode, we built a dataset consisting of 11,488 idea pairs. Through reinforcement learning (RLVR) and fine-tuning (SFT), an efficient small language model was shown to surpass the prediction performance of GPT-5. The Attribution Impossibility: No Feature Ranking Is Faithful, Stable, and Complete Under Collinearity When collinearity exists between features in an AI model, the feature importance ranking method proves the impossibility of satisfying reliability, stability, and completeness at the same time. If there is collinearity between features, deriving feature rankings is virtually no different from a random result. Existing methods are divided into two categories: 'reliable and complete but unstable methods' and 'stable ensemble methods', and there is no method that satisfies these requirements simultaneously. To solve this impossibility, the authors proposed the DASH (Diversified Aggregation of SHAP) technique and mechanically verified it through Lean 4. Don't Collapse Your Features: Why CenterLoss Hurts OOD Detection and Multi-Scale Mahalanobis Wins This study introduces GOEN, an outlier (OOD) detection technique for safe deployment of machine learning systems, and establishes that CenterLoss actually reduces OOD detection performance. Proposed GOEN pipeline combining multi-scale features, L2 regularization, and Mahalanobis distance for OOD detection. We find that using CenterLoss causes the feature space to be overly compressed, which reduces OOD detection performance. GOEN shows better OOD detection performance than existing techniques in the CIFAR-10 benchmark. HealthCraft: A Reinforcement Learning Safety Environment for Emergency Medicine 'HealthCraft', a new environment for assessing the safety of reinforcement learning-based language models in the field of emergency medicine, has been developed. It is designed to evaluate trajectory-level safety in emergency medicine settings, tool misuse, and failure modes in clinically stressful situations. FHIR R4 provides 195 operations and 2,255 binary evaluation criteria based on the world state. We confirmed that the performance of the current major language models (Claude Opus 4.6, GPT-5.4) collapsed to almost 0% in an actual multi-step emergency care workflow. Predicting Performance of Symbolic and Prompt Programs with Examples This paper studies a new model that predicts the performance of large-scale language model (LLM) prompted programs and symbol-based programs with only a small number of examples. The LLM prompt is unreliable because even if it passes a small number of tests, it may fail during actual deployment. Symbol-based programs have an ‘all or nothing’ characteristic, whereas prompted programs have a diffuse nature, with many ‘almost right’ results. Developed a 'RAP (Retrieved Approximate Prior)' technique that predicts performance by searching existing tasks and prompts. CR4T: Rewrite-Based Guardrails for Adolescent LLM Safety Taking into account the developmental characteristics of adolescents, we propose the CR4T safety framework, which rewrites harmful content into constructive advice instead of traditional safety mechanisms centered on rejection. The existing adult-centered and rejection-based LLM safety mechanism has limitations, such as the breakdown of conversation in youth interactions. CR4T is a model-independent safety framework that reframes harmful outputs into constructive guidance appropriate for youth's developmental level. Experiment results show that CR4T effectively manages harmful content and provides appropriate guidance while reducing unnecessary conversation blocking. Broadening Access to Transportation Safety Data with Generative AI: A Schema-Grounded Framework for Spatial Natural Language Queries Using generative AI, we propose a schema-based natural language query interface that allows even non-experts to easily view and analyze traffic safety data. Using LLM, we developed a framework that converts user intent into structured queries and executes them deterministically in the PostGIS database. By separating language interpretation and data execution, we ensure reproducibility and reliability of results and solve the problem of using AI in the public sector. As a result of evaluation using the Massachusetts traffic safety database, the validation layer proved effective by correcting 29% of query errors. Sem-Detect: Semantic Level Detection of AI Generated Peer-Reviews To distinguish between AI-written peer reviews and human-written reviews, we propose the 'Sem-Detect' technique, which combines text features and claim-level semantic analysis. It was developed with the understanding that while AI models converge on similar points, human reviewers provide more original and diverse opinions. Sem-Detect uses a method of analyzing target reviews by comparing them with multiple AI-generated reviews for the same paper. In tests on the ICLR and NeurIPS conference datasets, it showed a 25.5% performance improvement compared to the existing best model, and also effectively distinguishes human reviews refined with LLM. Probabilistic Attribution For Large Language Models We propose a new technique to probabilistically interpret and describe how each token contributes to the internal distribution of the model during the creation of a large-scale language model (LLM). Error 500 (Server Error)!!1500.That’s an error.There was an error. Please try again later.That’s all we know. Using Bayes' theorem, contribution is derived by calculating the response probability ratio when excluding and including specific tokens in the prompt. By analyzing the interaction of entropy and contribution scores, we identify uncertain and unstable parts of the model and improve interpretability. RankJudge: A Multi-Turn LLM-as-a-Judge Synthetic Benchmark Generator Research on RankJudge, a new benchmark system that generates intentionally error-injected dialogue pairs to evaluate the quality of multi-turn dialogues. Existing LLM evaluation benchmarks are focused on simple Q&A tasks and have limitations in evaluating complex multi-turn conversations. RankJudge accurately measures a model's evaluation ability by injecting a single fault into one turn of a conversation, creating unambiguous conversation pairs. We evaluated 21 LLM models in the fields of machine learning, biomedical, and finance and derived rankings using the Bradley-Terry model. Internship for early college students/applicants with no previous employment history Covers open source and research internship opportunities and preparation strategies for college freshmen or applicants without employment history. ‘Google Summer of Code (GSoC)’, where you can gain open source contribution experience, is a representative internship program recommended for freshmen. To successfully apply for an open source internship, it is important to actively interact with the project community in advance, and excessive use of LLM should be avoided. Research institutes such as MPI-SP and CISPA in Germany offer Research Intern programs for undergraduate students, and support is available for each research group. Microsoft begins reclaiming Claude Code licenses Microsoft has decided to license Claude Code from its in-house development team and unify its tools with the GitHub Copilot CLI. Microsoft is phasing out Claude Code for E+D teams responsible for Windows, M365, and more by the end of June. This action is interpreted as a strategic decision to encourage use of GitHub Copilot CLI, our tool, to unify workflow and reduce costs. A key challenge for Microsoft going forward is whether GitHub Copilot CLI, which will replace Claude Code, can provide the same level of productivity as before. The partnership with Anthropic will remain and the use of existing AI models will continue. Throwing a wall of AI-generated text into your conversation We point out the ‘Slop Grenade’ phenomenon, which impedes the efficiency of conversations by pasting unnecessarily long AI-generated answers in chat or email, and suggest ways to use AI correctly. Sending lengthy AI-generated answers to simple questions is an adversarial communication method that wastes the recipient’s time and disrupts the conversation. AI should not be used as a tool to increase the amount of meaningless information, but as a tool to clearly refine the human judgment of respondents. In conversation, more important than technical answers is communicating human judgment briefly and clearly. Building a Growth Engine from Zero: How Claude Code Increased ARR by 38% A single operator leveraged Claude Code to build a growth engine for a premium travel concierge service, growing ARR by 38% in six months. Without a dedicated growth team, we independently built the entire acquisition system from data analysis to marketing automation using Claude Code. By analyzing existing reservation data, we redefine our core customer base (ICP) and refine our brand message and targeting strategy accordingly. We build our own attribution rule engine on top of HubSpot to 100% track the performance of paid media and outbound channels and maximize operational efficiency. agentmemory - A persistent memory system for AI coding agents. This is an introduction to agentmemory, an open source memory system that enables persistent memory and context maintenance across sessions for AI coding agents. Tool usage history is automatically captured and compressed in the background and injected into subsequent sessions, eliminating the need to reinvent the stack every time. It combines BM25, Vector, and Graph triple stream searches to provide efficient information retrieval and uses a four-level memory integration model. It is compatible with various agents such as Claude Code and Cursor through MCP/REST and supports a real-time visualization viewer. NuExtract3 released: open-weight 4B VLM for Markdown, OCR and structured extraction (self-hostable) [P] Numind has released NuExtract3, an open-weight 4B VLM model specialized for extracting and structuring information from diverse documents. It is an Apache-2.0 license model based on Qwen3.5-4B and is optimized for recognizing complex document structures and extracting information such as PDFs, screenshots, and tables. You can convert document images to Markdown or extract data according to a target JSON template, and support local self-hosting. It provides various quantization versions to enable operation even with specifications as low as 4GB VRAM, and supports vLLM, SGLang, llama.cpp, etc. to optimize inference performance. Novel Problems in VLA [R] This is an article written by an intern researcher who is having difficulty finding original ideas in the field of VLA (Vision Language Action) model research, asking for advice from the community. Research interns are having difficulty coming up with original ideas due to saturation of VLA research topics. We successfully implemented Equivariant VLA based on Equivariant CNN announced in 2016, but found that existing research already exists. Requesting community advice on setting research directions and generating new research ideas. Live Human Detector on Outbound Phone Calls [R] Discussion of the development of a real-time voice classification tool that reduces agent connection latency by distinguishing between machine sounds and real agents in outbound phone calls. To streamline call center waiting times, the goal is to analyze audio during calls in real time to determine within 1-2 seconds whether a person has responded. Distinguishing between real human speech, recorded announcements (RVA), machine sounds, voice mail, and background noise is a technical challenge. We are considering a machine learning-based audio classification approach through waveform or spectrograph analysis without speech recognition (STT). Microsoft Cancels Internal Anthropic Licenses As Shift To Token-Based AI Billing Blows Up Annual Budgets In Months Microsoft canceled its internal Anthropic license due to early budget exhaustion due to token-based AI charging system. Microsoft has canceled its internal license for Anthropic AI. Due to the token-based AI charging system, the annual budget was exhausted much faster than expected. The cost of introducing AI technology is rapidly increasing to the point where even large companies feel burdened. Rethinking AI Bubble This article analyzes the current AI bubble theory, saying that it is unlikely that the bubble will burst any time soon, based on actual data center infrastructure and corporate value. We predict that the AI ​​bubble will not burst until OpenAI and Anthropic go public within the year. Unlike the DotCom bubble, AI companies now have massive data center infrastructure that has real value. The current valuations of OpenAI (35x) and Anthropic (13x) do not appear immediately unsustainable. Interesting Response from Gemini In this case, when a user asked why Google search quality was declining, Gemini provided an honest answer regarding its revenue structure. Users ask the search engine why the quality of Google searches is poor. Gemini responded honestly, mentioning the profit-generating structure. Users complain that search results are random and useless. AI training is becoming the new coding revolution As AI model training becomes more accessible, a new trend is emerging where even small development teams, not large corporations, can develop high-performance artificial intelligence in specific fields. AI model training, which was previously only possible for large corporations, is now possible at the individual developer level through GPU rental and open model fine-tuning. The key change is the removal of barriers to entry into AI research and development rather than the models themselves. The paradigm is shifting from an era of artificial intelligence construction monopolized by large corporations to an era in which small teams build more specialized artificial intelligence in specific fields. Multi-agent AI systems are now automating scientific discovery and nobody seems ready The content is that a multi-agent AI system that autonomously performs everything from hypothesis setting to experiment design and repeating results without human intervention is dramatically increasing the speed of scientific research. Two papers were recently published on AI systems that autonomously conduct scientific experiments without human approval. These systems have the potential to achieve speeds 100 to 1,000 times faster than existing research, overcoming human bandwidth limitations that have been a bottleneck in research. There are concerns that these changes are barely being covered in the mainstream media, despite the huge impact they will have on the biotech and drug discovery sectors. DeepSeek is pushing forward with $10.29 billion financing round, with Liang Wenfeng committing to continue developing open-source AI models rather than pursuing short-term commercialization goals DeepSeek is seeking to raise $10.29 billion in funding and has announced plans to focus on developing open source AI models rather than short-term monetization. DeepSeek is in the midst of a massive $10.29 billion funding round. Founder Liang Wenfeng emphasized that continuing to develop open source AI models takes precedence over short-term commercial goals. This investment is expected to focus on accelerating research and development to achieve artificial general intelligence (AGI) goals. When your LLM treats data center GPUs like an optional DLC A discussion in the IT community about LLM treating data center GPUs like optional DLC. Technical discussion on GPU resource utilization in LLM running environment Gap between high-performance hardware requirements and actual operating environment Awareness of GPU infrastructure dependency within the community [NEW] Supra-50M Released! SupraLabs has released 'Supra-50M', a lightweight language model with a 50M parameter scale. This is a 50M parameter base and instruction model designed based on the Llama structure. Trained on 20 billion high-quality educational web text tokens. Although it is very small compared to existing models, it shows competitive or superior performance in several benchmarks. [llama.cpp] Asymmetric KV q8/q4 cache: current caveats and discussion in GGML repo Discussion of performance degradation issues and solutions when using asymmetric KV cache quantization (q8/q4) in llama.cpp. When using mixed asymmetric KV cache quantization such as -ctk q8_0 -ctv q4_0 in llama.cpp, prompt handling switches to CPU rather than GPU in CUDA environment. To solve this problem, modifying the CUDA source or activating the compilation option GGML_CUDA_FA_ALL_QUANTS=ON is discussed, but the disadvantage is that the compilation time is very long. The community is discussing including a specific KV cache quantization combination when compiling, which can save more than half of memory with only a 1.3% precision loss compared to f16/f16. New Release of ROCm based MLX LLM Engine - lemon-mlx-engine lemon-mlx-engine has released a new version that integrates the latest ROCm 7.13 and includes bug and kernel fixes for Qwen series models. Lemon-mlx-engine integrates TheRock/ROCm 7.13, allowing you to use the latest ROCm features on local hardware. Various bugs and kernel issues related to Qwen3, 3.5, and 3.6 models (MoE and Dense) have been resolved. Users are encouraged to test the latest version. Dissecting ThunderKittens, anatomy of a compact DSL for high-performance AI kernels This article analyzes the characteristics and structure of 'ThunderKittens', an embedded DSL that balances productivity and hardware control for efficient GPU execution of AI models. The performance of modern machine learning models relies heavily on kernel implementations optimized for GPU hardware. ThunderKittens provides a new programming abstraction that bridges the gap between existing abstracted frameworks (PyTorch, Triton) and low-level control (CUDA C++). It is designed to automate repetitive hardware setup while providing precise control over memory placement and pipelining. First vaccines, now mammograms? RFK Jr.’s latest firings have doctors outraged. U.S. Secretary of Health Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has fired two vice chairs of the USPSTF, a preventive medicine panel, sparking strong opposition from the medical community. Secretary of Health Robert F. Kennedy Jr. fires two vice chairs of the United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF). Due to this dismissal and delay in appointing a successor, eight vacancies, or half of the 16-member panel, including the chairman and vice-chairman, were created. The task force has been unable to issue final recommendations on preventive healthcare and has been unable to hold meetings for the past year, which has paralyzed its functions. The $58,000 TV bill: When DirecTV sued O.J. Simpson for piracy DirecTV launched the 2005 O.J. This article covers a past case in which a lawsuit was filed against Simpson regarding illegal reception of satellite TV. In 2005, DirecTV launched a popular TV show called O.J. A satellite TV piracy (hacking) lawsuit was filed against Simpson. The lawsuit dealt with highly technical and complex issues, including satellite TV bootloaders and smart card voltage manipulation. The fact that celebrities used illegal reception techniques instead of paying was the core background of the lawsuit. 7 WFH gadgets that are huge quality of life improvements We recommend seven useful home office gadgets that can increase your work-from-home productivity and improve your quality of life. Introducing ergonomic keyboards, audio devices, and storage tools that can maximize productivity in a work-from-home environment. Recommended products include Jabra Evolve3 75 headphones, ProtoArc CaseUp combo, and ReMarkable Paper Pure. Most featured devices are available at discounted prices for Memorial Day. Thinking about plug-in solar? It may be coming to your state soon We cover the current status of legislation and possible deployment of small plug-in solar power systems that do not require professional installation in the United States. Plug-in solar power is a small solar power system that can be used immediately by plugging it into a regular household outlet. In the United States, Utah is currently the only state to have legalized it, and other states are considering introducing it as a model based on overseas examples such as Germany. It is an affordable alternative that allows homeowners as well as renters to save money on energy costs as it does not require professional installation or permitting procedures. This is the power backup setup I trust after years of testing - solar panels included We cover how to calculate your power storage needs and utilize portable power stations and solar panels to prepare for summer power outages. The possibility of power outages in the summer is increasing due to the rapid increase in air conditioner use, aging power grids, and extreme heat. We recommend using solar panels and portable power stations, which are eco-friendly and easy to maintain, which compensate for the shortcomings of gas generators. To secure stable power, you must accurately calculate your power usage requirements in advance and prepare preparedness measures. Uber Improves Restaurant Recommendations Using Real-Time Signals and Listwise Ranking Uber has updated its Uber Eats recommendation system to introduce real-time user signals and Listwise rankings to improve the restaurant discovery experience. Replace the batch-based feature pipeline with a real-time signal processing layer to reflect user intent, such as clicks and searches, in real time Introducing the Listwise ranking method to optimize the relative ranking among all options instead of evaluating candidate restaurants individually Reduce data drift and ensure consistency by applying the same feature extraction logic to offline learning and online serving environments InfoQ Launches Online AI Engineering Cohort and Certification for Senior Software Practitioners InfoQ has launched a new online AI engineering cohort training and certification program for senior software practitioners. We help senior engineers, architects, technical leads, etc. in making practical decisions related to building AI systems in production environments. It is a small, real-time cohort that takes place 4 hours a week for 5 weeks, and shares technical experience anonymously with industry peers. Hien Luu from Zoox will participate as an instructor and teach practice-based frameworks such as infrastructure, platform, and stability. Discord Rebuilds Database Operations Around Automation to Manage ScyllaDB at Massive Scale This is about the 'Scylla Control Plane (SCP)', an internal orchestration framework that Discord has built to automate the management of large-scale ScyllaDB clusters. Breaking away from the existing weak manual script method, we introduced SCP, a reusable task and workflow-oriented automation framework. Significantly reduce the operational burden on engineers by automating complex operational tasks such as rolling upgrades, cluster expansion, shadow cluster provisioning, and node recovery. Ensures operational stability through YAML-based declarative definitions, state persistence using SQLite, automated safety checks, and rollback protection. xAI Releases Grok Skills and Updates Tool Calling Responses API xAI has released the 'Grok Skills' feature and enhanced Responses API for Grok 4.3 models, allowing users and developers to build custom, reusable workflows. Grok Skills are personalized expertise capabilities that remember your workflow, preferences, and document processing routines and apply them continuously across the platform. The Responses API follows an OpenAI-compatible tool call format and supports native server-side execution, parallel tool calls, and a context window of 1 million tokens. It is differentiated from the agent functionality of existing third-party AI tools and focuses on lightweight, platform-native workflows through tight integration with the X platform. Presentation: AI Native Engineering Meta's Reality Labs introduces an example of reducing repetitive tasks and focusing on innovation by introducing AI-native engineering. Through the Assess and Grow framework, we present a maturity model that helps teams move from repetitive tasks to AI-integrated innovation. Demonstrate real-world results, such as achieving 90% code coverage, and address concerns about poor code quality and review fatigue. We aim to unleash the full potential of human creativity by transforming developers from simple builders to explorers and innovators. Megalodon GitHub Attack Targets 5,561 Repos with Malicious CI/CD Workflows An automated attack campaign called Megalodon stole cloud credentials and source code security information from 5,561 GitHub repositories by injecting malicious GitHub Actions workflows. The Megalodon campaign pushed 5,718 malicious commits to 5,561 repositories in 6 hours. By injecting a Base64 encoded Bash payload into a GitHub Actions workflow, credentials, tokens, SSH keys, etc. within the CI/CD environment were exfiltrated to the C2 server. The attackers used disposable accounts and forged author IDs to disguise routine CI maintenance. Making Vulnerable Drivers Exploitable Without Hardware - The BYOVD Perspective We cover methodologies for analyzing and attacking vulnerabilities in Windows kernel mode drivers even in a hardware-free environment. We present an analysis technique to determine whether hardware-dependent driver vulnerabilities can be accessed and exploited in user mode without hardware. Describes the key conditions for BYOVD (Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver) attacks and how to analyze the attack surface through Device Objects. Describes how driver vulnerabilities can be used to defeat system security defenses. Cohere launches ‘Command A+’, an agent model running on two H100 units Cohere has unveiled ‘Command A+’, a highly efficient open source multi-modal LLM optimized for enterprise agent workflow. It is an open source model under the Apache 2.0 license specialized in enterprise agent tasks, RAG, and multimodal document processing. By adopting a sparse MoE structure, we maximize computational efficiency by activating only 25 billion of the total 218 billion parameters during actual inference. The lightest version of W4A4 is designed to run with two H100 GPUs or one Blackwell GPU. Hancom and LG AI Research Institute take the lead in targeting the public market with ‘ChatExaOne’ Hancom will jointly promote an integrated AI solution business for public and private markets by installing its AI agent on LG AI Research Institute's 'ChatExaOne' platform. Hancom and LG AI Research Institute will integrate Hancom's document AI agent into 'ChatExaOne' through a strategic partnership. Beyond simple summaries, it provides differentiated functions that allow you to directly create, edit, and save Hangul document formats. The two companies will cooperate in overall AI business, including joint targeting of public markets and on-device AI and document automation. [Bulletin Board] Weverse Brain, joins SK ‘K-AI Alliance’, etc. This is a short article on major trends of domestic AI companies, technological cooperation, education programs, and AI use cases in the defense field. Weverse Brain has joined the 'K-AI Alliance' led by SK Group to promote technology exchange and joint projects. Dayone Company's Kernel Academy is recruiting participants for 'Uniquer', a supporter-type hackathon for college students. Synapsoft held a high-quality data parsing seminar for autonomous AI agent workflow. Dtonic introduced AI technology that supports battlefield situation awareness and decision-making at the ‘2026 Advanced Defense Pitch Day’. Open AI surpasses Antropic in sales in the first quarter...allowed for a reversal in the second quarter Open AI recorded higher sales than Antropic in the first quarter, but is expected to lose its lead from the second quarter due to Antropic's rapid growth. OpenAI surpassed Antropic with sales of $5.7 billion in the first quarter, but the sales lead is expected to be reversed due to Antropic's rapid growth. Open AI is showing a loss structure due to huge investment costs, but Antropic expects to increase sales and even turn into a surplus in the second quarter. Open AI is pursuing a long-term advertising business strategy by expanding users and paid subscribers through 'GPT-5.5' and the low-cost plan 'ChatGPT Go'. Ministry of Food and Drug Safety to hold ‘Advanced AI Medical Product Red Team Challenge’ in September… Promote advancement of medical AI verification system The Ministry of Food and Drug Safety will hold the 'Advanced AI Medical Product Red Team Challenge' in September to verify the safety and reliability of medical AI products. The Red Team Challenge and technical workshop to check vulnerabilities and risk factors in medical AI products will be held from September 3 to 4. This year's challenge is targeting a total of 15 types of LLM and medical specialized models, which is 1.5 times larger than last year. The ‘Generative AI Digital Medical Product Red Team Test and Evaluation Platform’ developed as an R&D project by the Ministry of Food and Drug Safety will be applied for the first time to this event. [ZD SW Today] Megazone Cloud participates in AWS Summit Seoul 2026, etc. We report on the introduction and cooperation of AI technology by SW and cloud companies such as Megazone Cloud, Cloudflare, and Detonic. Megazone Cloud unveiled its agentic AI strategy and enterprise AI orchestrator solution at AWS Summit Seoul 2026. Cloudflare has teamed up with Antropic to launch a dedicated environment for running cloud-managed agents. DeTonic demonstrated practical battlefield recognition and decision support technology using an AI data platform at the ‘2026 Advanced Defense Pitch Day’. Google, not Google anymore... 6 search engines that emerged as alternatives It covers user resistance caused by Google's complete reorganization of search to focus on AI, and six search engines that can replace it. As Google reorganizes its search engine to focus on conversational AI, it is forcing AI functions into all search results, causing dissatisfaction among some users. The inaccuracy of past AI answers and Google's monopoly issues are causing 'Google fatigue', and an increasing number of users are preferring traditional search methods. The six search engines presented as alternatives (Kagi, DuckDuckGo, StartPage, &udm=14, Brave, and Ecosia) are characterized by the ability to selectively control AI functions. Com2uS Platform, heated consultation at ‘2026 Play Expo’ B2B booth… “Global service support” Com2uS Platform supports developers' global expansion by introducing game back-end service 'Hive' and Tencent Cloud collaboration AI service at '2026 Play Expo'. Provides global operation specialized functions such as support for 16 languages ​​and payment linkage through game back-end service (GBaaS) ‘Hive’ Relieves the burden of introduction for small and medium-sized developers by applying an on-demand rate plan that sets costs based on usage Collaborating with Tencent Cloud to release a MaaS-based AI package that can utilize multiple generative AI models with a single API"